1
00:00:07,360 --> 00:00:12,540
A gardener wants a rectangular flower bed, 6 metres by 3 and a half metres.

2
00:00:12,590 --> 00:00:15,890
She has string, pegs and a tape measure.

3
00:00:15,940 --> 00:00:18,440
She measures the 4 sides correctly.

4
00:00:18,490 --> 00:00:21,910
But are the corners really right angles?

5
00:00:21,960 --> 00:00:26,140
Which property guarantees that the corners are right angles?

6
00:00:26,190 --> 00:00:29,450
Pause the video and choose.

7
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:32,370
The answer is: the two diagonals are equal.

8
00:00:32,420 --> 00:00:37,880
A shape with the correct sides can still lean over, like a parallelogram.

9
00:00:37,930 --> 00:00:45,250
But when the two diagonals are the same length, the shape must be a rectangle, with 4 right angles.

10
00:00:45,300 --> 00:00:47,450
Remember how to use a protractor.

11
00:00:47,500 --> 00:00:50,810
Put its centre on the vertex of the angle.

12
00:00:50,860 --> 00:00:53,880
Line up zero with one arm of the angle.

13
00:00:53,930 --> 00:00:57,410
Then read from the scale that starts at zero.

14
00:00:57,460 --> 00:01:04,390
An acute angle must be less than 90 degrees, so if you read 130 and 50, choose 50.

15
00:01:04,440 --> 00:01:08,630
To draw a line perpendicular to another line, use a set square.

16
00:01:08,680 --> 00:01:13,150
Put one edge along the line, and draw along the other edge.

17
00:01:13,200 --> 00:01:15,270
You can also use a protractor.

18
00:01:15,320 --> 00:01:19,280
Put the centre on the point, and mark 90 degrees.

19
00:01:19,330 --> 00:01:25,570
To draw parallel lines, slide the set square along a ruler, and draw a line at each position.

20
00:01:25,620 --> 00:01:28,750
The lines stay the same distance apart.

21
00:01:28,800 --> 00:01:29,440
Your turn.

22
00:01:29,490 --> 00:01:33,920
On a diagram, what do lines marked with matching arrows tell you?

23
00:01:33,970 --> 00:01:37,190
Pause the video and choose.

24
00:01:37,240 --> 00:01:40,220
Matching arrows mean the lines are parallel.

25
00:01:40,270 --> 00:01:46,640
Small squares in a corner mean a right angle, and matching dashes mean equal lengths.

26
00:01:46,690 --> 00:01:50,480
To draw a quadrilateral accurately, draw one side first.

27
00:01:50,530 --> 00:01:55,570
Then use a protractor for each angle, and a ruler for each length.

28
00:01:55,620 --> 00:01:57,360
Take this parallelogram.

29
00:01:57,410 --> 00:02:00,460
Draw the base, 6 centimetres long.

30
00:02:00,510 --> 00:02:04,280
At the left end, measure an angle of 63 degrees.

31
00:02:04,330 --> 00:02:07,850
At the right end, measure 117 degrees.

32
00:02:07,900 --> 00:02:12,180
Mark the sloping sides to the same length, and join the top.

33
00:02:12,230 --> 00:02:17,200
Notice that the two neighbouring angles add up to 180 degrees.

34
00:02:17,250 --> 00:02:18,530
Here is the method.

35
00:02:18,580 --> 00:02:24,020
Step 1: make a quick sketch, and label every length and angle you know.

36
00:02:24,070 --> 00:02:28,180
Step 2: draw the longest side you know with a ruler.

37
00:02:28,230 --> 00:02:34,500
Step 3: measure each angle at its ends with a protractor, and draw the next sides.

38
00:02:34,550 --> 00:02:40,050
Step 4: mark the lengths, join up, and check your drawing by measuring.

39
00:02:40,100 --> 00:02:43,650
Draw a rectangle 7 centimetres by 4 centimetres.

40
00:02:43,700 --> 00:02:47,510
First draw the side A B, 7 centimetres long.

41
00:02:47,560 --> 00:02:51,090
At A and at B, draw lines perpendicular to A B.

42
00:02:51,140 --> 00:02:53,900
Mark a point 4 centimetres up each line.

43
00:02:53,950 --> 00:02:56,140
Call the points D and C.

44
00:02:56,190 --> 00:02:56,970
Join D to C.

45
00:02:57,020 --> 00:03:02,690
Check: D C should be 7 centimetres long, and the diagonals should be equal.

46
00:03:02,740 --> 00:03:06,660
Amir draws a parallelogram with an angle of 50 degrees.

47
00:03:06,710 --> 00:03:09,510
What should the other angles be?

48
00:03:09,560 --> 00:03:12,920
In a parallelogram, opposite angles are equal.

49
00:03:12,970 --> 00:03:16,130
So the opposite angle is 50 degrees.

50
00:03:16,180 --> 00:03:19,990
Neighbouring angles add up to 180 degrees.

51
00:03:20,040 --> 00:03:21,830
180 minus 50 is 130.

52
00:03:21,880 --> 00:03:26,230
The other two angles are 130 degrees each.

53
00:03:26,280 --> 00:03:26,950
Your turn.

54
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:30,050
A parallelogram has an angle of 64 degrees.

55
00:03:30,100 --> 00:03:32,140
What is the angle next to it?

56
00:03:32,190 --> 00:03:35,130
Pause and work it out.

57
00:03:35,180 --> 00:03:40,530
Neighbouring angles in a parallelogram add up to 180 degrees.

58
00:03:40,580 --> 00:03:43,560
180 minus 64 is 116 degrees.

59
00:03:43,610 --> 00:03:46,800
Kim draws an angle of 40 degrees at point A.

60
00:03:46,850 --> 00:03:49,410
Which line goes wrong first?

61
00:03:49,460 --> 00:03:50,630
Line 2 is wrong.

62
00:03:50,680 --> 00:03:56,520
Kim read the wrong scale, so the angle she drew is really 140 degrees.

63
00:03:56,570 --> 00:04:01,000
Always use the scale that starts at zero on the arm of the angle.

64
00:04:01,050 --> 00:04:06,710
And check: 40 degrees is acute, so it must look smaller than a right angle.

65
00:04:06,760 --> 00:04:10,490
Builders need perfect right angles for walls and floors.

66
00:04:10,540 --> 00:04:13,540
Here is a trick they still use today.

67
00:04:13,590 --> 00:04:17,580
They make a triangle with sides of 3, 4 and 5 units.

68
00:04:17,630 --> 00:04:21,620
For example, 3 metres, 4 metres and 5 metres.

69
00:04:21,670 --> 00:04:27,270
The corner between the 3 side and the 4 side is always exactly 90 degrees.

70
00:04:27,320 --> 00:04:29,430
No protractor needed.

71
00:04:29,480 --> 00:04:30,630
One last question.

72
00:04:30,680 --> 00:04:33,300
What is true about the angles of a kite?

73
00:04:33,350 --> 00:04:36,510
Pause the video and choose.

74
00:04:36,560 --> 00:04:39,370
A kite has one pair of equal opposite angles.

75
00:04:39,420 --> 00:04:43,480
It also has two pairs of equal sides next to each other.

76
00:04:43,530 --> 00:04:50,200
To remember: perpendicular lines meet at 90 degrees, and parallel lines never meet.

77
00:04:50,250 --> 00:04:57,730
Use a set square or a protractor for perpendicular lines, and a sliding set square for parallel lines.

78
00:04:57,780 --> 00:05:04,030
To draw a quadrilateral: sketch, draw one side, measure the angles, mark the lengths.

79
00:05:04,080 --> 00:05:11,940
Check with what you know: equal diagonals, equal opposite angles, and neighbours that add up to 180.

80
00:05:11,990 --> 00:05:17,030
Great work! Now practise lesson 5.3 on Camday.
